  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golovinomyces verbenae is a powdery mildew fungus that infects verbena plants, causing a white, powdery coating on leaves and stems. This obligate parasite is common in New England gardens and landscapes, particularly in warm, dry conditions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Dense white to grayish powder covers affected leaf surfaces, stems, and sometimes flowers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "Primarily infects verbena species and related plants in the Verbenaceae family",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic Structures",
      "description": "Presence of distinctive appendages on spores visible under magnification helps confirm species identification",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golovinomyces verbenae thrives on verbena plants in gardens, landscapes, and disturbed areas throughout New England, favoring warm days, cool nights, and moderate humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many fungi, powdery mildews do not require water droplets to spread—they can infect plants in dry conditions, making them particularly troublesome in drought years.",
